readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|verb, ''as'' meaning ‘after the manner of,’ ‘the same as,’ ‘like,’ ‘in the character or capacity of,’ etc.: as, the audience rose ''as'' one man; all these things were ''as'' nothing to him; he has been nominated ''as'' a candidate. Hence in constructions where the appositive clause depends directly upon the noun: as, his career ''as'' a soldier was brilliant; his reputation ''as'' a scholar stands high: and so in naming phases of a general subject: as, Washington ''as'' a general; man ''as'' a thinker. The construction as a quasi-predicate appositive or factitive object after a principal verb is usual after verbs of seeming or regarding.}}

{{smaller block|Evil was embrac’d for good, wickedness honour’d and esteem’d ''as'' virtue.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., iii.}}}}

{{smaller block|That law which concerneth men ''as'' men.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Pol.}}}}

{{smaller block|This gentleman was known to his contemporaries ''as'' a man of fortune, and ''as'' the author of two successful plays.
{{right|''Macaulay''.}}}}

{{smaller block|Our age is bewailed ''as'' the age of Introversion.
{{right|''Emerson'', Amer. Scholar.}}}}

{{smaller block|The subordinate clause introduced by ''as'' is often not dependent grammatically upon the principal verb, but serves to restrict or determine the scope of the statement as a whole. Such clauses are parenthetical, and usually elliptical, some of them, as ''as usual'' and ''as a rule'', having almost the idiomatical unity of an adverbial phrase.}}

{{smaller block|The streets were narrow, ''as'' is usual in Moorish and Arab cities.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|In certain emphatic formulas, ''as'' (‘even as’) introduces a solemn attestation (‘as truly or surely as’) or adjuration (‘in a manner befitting the fact that’), approaching a causal sense, ‘since, because.’ (See 2, below.)}}

{{smaller block|But truly ''as'' the Lord liveth, and ''as'' thy soul liveth, there is but a step between me and death.
{{right|1 Sam. xx. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Now, ''as'' you are a Roman, tell me true.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., iv. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>''As'' ever thou lov’st valour, or wear’st arms
To punish baseness, shew it!</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Knight of Malta, iv. 4.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''2.''' Of reason: Since; because; inasmuch as.
{{smaller block|He who would persuade us of his sorrow for the sins of other men, ''as'' they are sins, not ''as'' they are sin’d against himself, must give us first some testimony of a sorrow for his own sins.
{{right|''Milton'', Eikonoklastes, viii.}}}}
{{smaller block|''As'' the wind was favorable, I had an opportunity of surveying this amazing scene.
{{right|''Bp. Berkeley.''}}}}

'''3.''' Of time: When; while; during the time that.
{{smaller block|<poem>And whistled ''as'' he went for want of thought.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Cymon and Iphigenia, l. 85.}}}}
{{smaller block|''As'' day broke, the scene of slaughter unfolded its horrors.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 97.}}}}

'''4.''' Of purpose or result: The consequent in the correlations ''so . . . as'', ''such . . . as'': To such a degree that; in such a manner that: followed by an infinitive or, formerly, by a finite verb (but in the latter construction ''that'' has taken the place of ''as'').
{{smaller block|''So'' many examples ''as'' filled xv. bookes.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 157.}}}}
{{smaller block|The relations are ''so'' uncertain ''as'' they require a great deal of examination.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|Indeed the prospect of affairs here is ''so'' strange and melancholy, ''as'' would make any one desirous of withdrawing from the country at any rate.
{{right|''Hume.''}}}}
{{smaller block|With a depth ''so'' great ''as'' to make it a day’s journey from the rear to the van, and a front ''so'' narrow ''as'' to consist of one gun and one horseman.
{{right|''Kinglake'', Crimea, III. ix.}}}}

'''5.''' Of mere continuation, introducing a clause in explanation or amplification of a word or statement in the principal clause, especially in giving examples: For example; for instance; to wit; thus.
{{smaller block|Winter birds, ''as'' woodcocks and fieldfares.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|A simple idea is one idea; ''as'' sweet, bitter.
{{right|''Locke.''}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
'''6.''' In dependent clauses: That. {{smaller|Formerly ''as'' was often attached, like ''that'', to the adverbs ''there'', ''then'', ''where'', ''when'', etc., to make them distinctly relative. These forms are now obsolete, except ''whereas'', which remains in a deflected sense. See ''whereas''. From this interchange with ''that'' followed the use of ''as'' for ''that'', in introducing an object clause after ''say'', ''know'', ''think'', etc., varying with ''as that'' and ''as how'': only in dialectal use: as, I don’t know ''as'' I do′, and I don’t know ''as''′ I do, the sense varying with the accent. [Colloq., New Eng.]}}
{{smaller block|“Dunnow’z I know:” the nearest your true Yankee ever comes to acknowledging ignorance.
{{right|''Lowell'', Introd. to Biglow Papers, 2d ser.}}}}

'''7.''' After comparatives: Than. [Now only prov. Eng.; cf. G. ''als'', the regular construction after comparatives.]
{{smaller block|How may the herte be more contryte and meke ''as'' whan of very contrycion . . . we aske mercy and forgyueness of almyghty god?
{{right|''Bp. Fisher'', I. 210.}}
Darkness itself is no more opposite to light ''as'' their actions were diametricall to their words.
{{right|''Howell'', Parly of Beasts, p. 48.}}
I rather like him ''as'' otherwise. ''Scott'', St. Ronan’s Well.}}

'''8.''' Before certain adverbs and adverbial phrases, including prepositional phrases: Even; just: restricting the application to a particular point: as, ''as'' now, ''as'' then, ''as'' yet, ''as'' here, ''as'' there, etc. [Now only dialectal, except ''as yet''. See phrases below.]
{{smaller block|There is no Christian duty that is not to be seasoned and set off with cheerishness—which in a thousand outward}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|and intermitting crosses may yet be done well, ''as'' in this vale of tears.
{{right|''Milton.''}}}}

{{smaller block|Before punishment he was to be heard ''as'' to-morrow.
{{right|''Warburton.''}}}}

Before prepositional phrases ''as'' becomes attached in thought to the preposition, making practically a new prepositional unit. See ''as anent'', ''as concerning'', ''as for'', etc., below.—'''As anent, as concerning, as touching,''' more commonly '''as for, as to,''' so far as it concerns; as regards; as respects; in regard to; in respect to: introducing a particular point or subject of thought.

{{smaller block|<poem>And in regard of causes now in hand,
Which I have open’d to his grace at large,
''As touching'' France.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Sir A.'' Jack! Jack! what think you of blooming, love-breathing seventeen?

''Abs.'' ''As to'' that, sir, I am quite indifferent. If I can please you in the matter, ’tis all I desire.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, iii. 1.}}}}

'''As if, as though,''' as it would be if, or supposing that.

{{smaller block|It seemed to the affrighted inhabitants ''as if'' the fiends of the air had come upon the wings of the wind, and possessed themselves of tower and turret.
{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 21.}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>Blinded alike from sunshine and from rain,
''As though'' a rose should shut and be a bud again.</poem>
{{right|''Keats'', Eve of St. Agnes.}}}}

'''As it were,''' as if it were so; in some sort; so to speak: a parenthetical clause indicating that a statement or comparison is admitted to be inexact, though substantially correct for the purpose intended.

{{smaller block|Brutus, that expell’d the kings out of Rome, was for the time forc’t to be, ''as it were'', a king himself till matters were set in order as in a free Commonwealth.
{{right|''Milton'', Prelatical Episcopacy.}}}}

'''As much,''' what amounts to the same thing; the same.

{{smaller block|<poem>But if you laugh at my rude carriage
In peace, I’ll do ''as much'' for you in war</poem>}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>When you come thither.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid’s Tragedy, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|'''As well (as)''', just as much (as); equally (with); in addition (to); besides: as, that is true, but it is true of the other ''as well''; this is the case with manufacturing ''as well as'' with agricultural interests.}}

{{smaller block|In order to convict Peacham it was necessary to find facts ''as well as'' law. {{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Bacon.}}}}

{{smaller|'''As who''', as one who; as if one: as, ''as who'' should say.—'''As yet''', so far; up to this time; hitherto.—'''Forasmuch, inasmuch.''' See these words.}}

'''III.''' ''rel. pron.'' That; who; which: after ''such'' or ''same'', and introducing an attributive clause: as, he did not look for ''such'' a result ''as'' that; he traveled the ''same'' route ''as'' I did.

{{smaller block|They fear religion with ''such'' a fear ''as'' loves not.
{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, ii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|[In this use also formerly after ''that, this, what''; now dialectally or vulgarly also after a personal pronoun, or, by omission, as a simple relative dependent on a noun.
<poem>That gentleness
And show of love ''as'' I was wont to have.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 2.}}
<poem>Under these hard conditions, ''as'' this time
Is like to lay upon us.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., i. 2.}}
<poem>Here I do bequeathe to thee
In full possession half that Kendal hath,
And what ''as'' Bradford holds of me in chief.</poem>
{{right|''Old Play'' (Dodsley, II. 47).}}
Take the box ''as'' stands in the first fire-place.
{{right|''Dickens'', Pickwick Papers.]}}}}

